WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:05.247
""Hereditary"" has been called,

00:00:05.247 --> 00:00:07.256
"The Scariest Movie of the Year"

00:00:08.997 --> 00:00:09.880
- Mom?

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:11.746
- I don't like this.
Dad, I don't like this.

00:00:11.746 --> 00:00:12.637
- What's Happening?

00:00:12.637 --> 00:00:13.470
- Peanut!

00:00:15.104 --> 00:00:16.380
- [Narrator] And one reason for that

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:18.820
is these creepy model houses.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:21.160
- In this movie, the models almost became

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.030
a character in the movie in a sense.

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:24.410
- [Narrator] Steve Newburn is the owner

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:27.400
of Applied Arts FX Studio in Canada.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:28.690
He's done visual effects for

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:30.890
The Dark Knight Rises and Inception.

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:32.460
- My team and I, we built all the

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.510
prosthetics and models for "Hereditary".

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:35.890
- [Narrator] If you haven't seen it yet,

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:37.970
turn back now because there's some

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:40.140
possible spoilers ahead.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.470
Minature models are used
all the time in movies.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:44.790
Filmed in a way to make them look

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:46.410
like a life sized object.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:47.540
- Usually with miniature work,

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:48.609
the intent is not to see it.

00:00:48.609 --> 00:00:50.910
It's intended to to
duplicate a real thing.

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:53.040
- [Narrator] But in
"Hereditary", the miniatures

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.960
were actually part of the story.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.110
Toni Collette's character
creates the models

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:58.930
in preparation for a gallery.

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:00.360
Steve and his crew were tasked

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.100
with making about 15 of them

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.140
to match the life sized film sets.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.950
- We had a thousand pieces at one point

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.730
and really didn't know exactly
how they went together.

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:10.790
- [Narrator] The whole process took

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:12.490
quite a bit of time to complete.

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:16.290
- Ten weeks with a crew of
about half a dozen people.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:17.220
- [Narrator] The buildings included

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.900
a funeral home, a preschool, a hospice

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:22.680
and an accident scene.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:24.100
The largest of the models was a

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:25.880
copy of the family home.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:28.950
And it's exteriors were
modeled after a real house.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:31.130
- Yeah, I went down in 6-8 weeks

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:32.780
before they started filming and

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:34.150
just kind of surveyed the house.

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:36.030
Took several hundred photos,

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:37.600
a bunch of measurements,
that sort of thing.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:38.780
- [Narrator] He worked with the director

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:40.580
to nail down all the details.

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:42.370
- Flooring, they'd sent us samples,

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:44.210
wallpaper, they would send up samples.

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:46.400
All that stuff would be
taken and scaled down

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.280
to the appropriate scale.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:49.160
- [Narrator] The house was built

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:50.960
in nine separate pieces.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:52.920
- We built the entire
thing as a giant puzzle

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.410
where you could remove sections of it

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:56.840
to shoot from that location.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:57.890
- [Narrator] The models that didn't need

00:01:57.890 --> 00:01:59.610
to resemble the set, were built using

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:01.950
generic images online as a guide.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.280
Like the preschool.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:05.130
All of the furniture was either handmade

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.710
or 3D printed depending on the complexity.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:08.870
- If they were a wood finish,

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:10.700
odds are we actually made the battle wood.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:12.620
If they were something that was metal

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:17.370
or super detailed, 3D printed quite often.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:18.610
- [Narrator] They had multiple in-house

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:21.100
3D printers working simultaneously.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:23.090
While outsourcing some of the work.

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:24.170
They also had to create some

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.480
creepy looking figurines
of the characters.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.620
- [Steve] The people were,
they were all 3D printed.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:29.580
Some of them were sculpted.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.860
Toni Collette for
example, we took that from

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.630
a digital scan of Toni we did in New York.

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:37.510
The grandmother was a
scan of somebody else.

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:38.440
- [Narrator] The car used in

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:40.480
the most disturbing scene in the movie

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:42.730
also had to look just
like the one in the film.

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:45.390
- [Steve ] The car we
modeled and 3D printed.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.590
It was a Volvo, I believe it was a V70.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:50.820
It's the exact model car
they used in the movie.

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:52.270
- [Narrator] Some of
the models were finished

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:54.070
and then taken apart to make them look

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:56.260
like they were in progress.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:57.590
- There was a lot of pieces of furniture,

00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:00.347
for example, that we
made that we just like,

00:03:00.347 --> 00:03:01.620
let's leave that out on the side

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:03.130
and it's on a shelf somewhere.

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:04.930
She hasn't quite finished
it. That kind of thing.

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:06.160
- [Narrator] Another important set piece

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:08.570
was a house sinking into the ground.

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:09.990
It was made with card foam

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.130
and covered in real dirt and soil.

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:14.220
- [Steve] Supposed to be
showcasing time passing.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:15.880
And like, these things have been sinking

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:18.270
into the ground like
quicksand kind of thing.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:19.860
And as they get further down,

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:21.710
they're also a lot more deteriorated.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.030
- [Narrator] After all the hard work,

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:28.470
many of the models were ultimately

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:30.620
destroyed by Toni Collette's character

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:32.560
as she starts breaking down.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.500
- Don't you ever raise your voice at me!

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:35.700
I am your mother!

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:37.260
- They were all built to be smashed.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:38.100
- [Narrator] Steve says that

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:39.520
they actually filmed the scene

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:40.570
for smashing the models,

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:42.560
which ended up being cut from the film.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:43.910
- Some of the ones that
were actually intended

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:45.260
to be break away and come apart

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:46.800
and get squished were built out

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.090
of basswood and balsa wood.

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:50.300
Other ones that weren't
intended to be smashed

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:52.950
where MBF which is a high density fiber,

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:55.280
like cardboard kind of wood.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:56.500
- [Narrator] But Steve says watching his

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:58.518
masterpieces get destroyed is all

00:03:58.518 --> 00:04:00.340
just part of the job.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:01.430
- It's kind of run of the mill

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:02.770
for the miniature side of things.

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:04.120
Most of the time when
you're building a miniature,

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:06.180
it's for the purpose of it being blown up,

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:09.650
or smashed, or destroyed in some respect.

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:10.680
It's actually the thing

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:11.750
you kind of look forward to.

